# No GitHub Actions - use local scripts for every CI/CD step

This repo does NOT rely on GitHub Actions. The account has been billing-
blocked more than once (Oct 2026) and every workflow died in 2 seconds with
`"The job was not started because recent account payments have failed..."`.
Even once billing is restored, the policy is: **do not add new workflows,
do not depend on existing ones to gate merges or ship releases.**

## What runs where

| Concern              | Script (run locally)                              |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Typecheck (web)      | `cd apps/web && npm run typecheck`                |
| Typecheck (mobile)   | `cd apps/mobile && bash scripts/mobile-typecheck.sh` |
| Build web image      | implicit inside `scripts/deploy_vke.sh`            |
| Push images to VKR   | implicit inside `scripts/deploy_vke.sh`            |
| Apply + rollout      | implicit inside `scripts/deploy_vke.sh`            |
| Create GitHub Release| `python3 scripts/publish_release.py` (also auto-  |
|                      | invoked at the end of `scripts/deploy_vke.sh`)    |
| Version bump         | `python3 scripts/bump_pr_version.py` (ONCE per PR) |
| Merge PR             | `gh pr merge <N> --squash --admin --delete-branch` |

## Rules for the agent

1. **Never author a new `.github/workflows/*.yml`.** If a workflow seems
 needed, write a shell script under `scripts/` instead. The user will run
 it from their terminal (or wire it to a `cron`/`launchd` later).
2. **Never cite "waiting for CI" as a reason to delay a merge.** CI will
 never go green on this repo. Admin-squash-merge after local gates pass.
3. **Never remove `scripts/publish_release.py` or the trailing call to it
 inside `scripts/deploy_vke.sh`.** That pair is the only way GitHub
 Releases stay in sync with `VERSION` + `CHANGELOG.txt`.
4. **When bumping the version, use the standard flow:** edit code → run
 `python3 scripts/bump_pr_version.py` ONCE → add CHANGELOG entry naming
 the new version → commit → push → `gh pr create --base main` → admin-
 merge → run `bash scripts/deploy_vke.sh` (which now also publishes the
 release).
5. **If a legacy workflow fires anyway and fails,** that's fine - the
 billing block is the right outcome. Don't try to fix the workflow YAML.
6. **Monitoring (synthetic-monitor, cluster-status) is a user-run concern,
 not an Actions concern.** If the user wants a probe, add it as a shell
 script invokable from cron/launchd on their machine, not as a workflow.

## Rationale

GitHub Actions is a shared, external, billable system that has repeatedly
blocked us at the worst time. Local scripts are:
- Deterministic (same machine, same `.venv`, same `.zshrc`).
- Debuggable in-shell with the full environment the user already has.
- Free (no runner minutes, no spend-limit anxiety).
- Portable (anyone with the repo and credentials can ship).

If you catch yourself typing `.github/workflows/` or `gh workflow run`,
stop. Write a script in `scripts/` and call it from the user's shell.
